    {
      "id": 224818,
      "name": "KateWalker",
      "username": "TakeWalker",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/takewalker/{size}/5677_2.png",
      "created_at": "2019-02-07T23:02:23.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"jlabrecque42\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/j/ecccb3/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> jlabrecque42:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Metagaming this system will just make for a boring story.&nbsp; Maybe turn it around on them.&nbsp; Create your own metagame where each of the comic writers are competing for issue sales.&nbsp; The hero with the best role playing, most creative use of powers, and overcomming to hardest failure us able to sell the most issues that month. Kind of a game outside the game. <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I remember some folks being discouraged by early descriptions of the RPG as “you’re a bunch of comic writers working on a story” rather than “you’re a bunch of super heroes trying to stop a crime”, but this is some seriously excellent advice. I’d say it’s also proof you can approach the system either way.</p>",
